{
  "format": "srwb",
  "version": "1.0",
  "notebook": {
    "name": "Breakfast Democracy: R → Python → Prolog",
    "cells": [
      {
        "code": "# Breakfast Democracy\n\nCan a group prefer **A over B**, **B over C**, and still prefer **C over A**?\n\nThis workbook follows a published breakfast-preference dataset through three languages:\n\n1. **R** downloads and validates 42 complete rankings of 15 foods, then plots first choices.\n2. **Python** verifies the data's SHA-256, groups ranking vectors with a small deterministic k-means model, and writes pairwise facts to a named source cell.\n3. **Prolog** reads that source cell and searches for a rock–paper–scissors-style majority cycle.\n\n## Run order and requirements\n\nRun **fetch_breakfast_votes → learn_preferences → find_majority_cycle**. The intervening `pairwise_model` cell is a named data/source carrier; Python overwrites it, and you do not need to run it after generation. Running all cells from top to bottom also works.\n\nSciREPL supplies base R, NumPy, and the cross-kernel shared-filesystem and named-cell APIs. The first R run needs internet access to **raw.githubusercontent.com** for one pinned public data file (about 2.6 KB). If the app requests network consent or a host allowlist entry, review that request. No downloaded text is executed as code, no AI service is called, and no API key is needed. Initial runtime installation can require separate downloads.\n\nR writes `/shared/breakfast_source.soc`, `breakfast_ballots.csv`, and `breakfast_foods.csv`; Python updates only the named `pairwise_model` cell. A saved SHA-256-verified source file is reused on later runs, so those runs can work offline. Do not rename the data carrier or change the dataset's column names.\n\nThe data are the **juice, cold cereal, beverage** context from Green & Rao (1972), digitized by PrefLib. These 42 respondents are a historical convenience sample, not a representative survey of present-day breakfast tastes.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "intro"
      },
      {
        "code": "# This immutable source pin is data, not executable R/Python/Prolog code.\nurl <- \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PrefLib/PrefLib-Data/6e2e9dcef5ee624383e3e2031f741728dd64d032/datasets/00035%20-%20breakfast/00035-00000004.soc\"\nsource_file <- \"/shared/breakfast_source.soc\"\nexpected_md5 <- \"75c0e89696e16ac7e6bb2a946943f529\"  # canonical LF text; base-R integrity check\n\n# Reuse a validated cached source. Never silently fall back to different data.\ncached_ok <- file.exists(source_file) &&\n  identical(unname(tools::md5sum(source_file)), expected_md5)\nif (!cached_ok) {\n  lines <- readLines(url, warn = FALSE, encoding = \"UTF-8\")\n  writeLines(lines, source_file, useBytes = TRUE)\n  if (!identical(unname(tools::md5sum(source_file)), expected_md5))\n    stop(\"Pinned dataset hash mismatch; stop and inspect the source.\")\n}\nlines <- readLines(source_file, warn = FALSE, encoding = \"UTF-8\")\n\nname_lines <- grep(\"^# ALTERNATIVE NAME\", lines, value = TRUE)\nfood_ids <- as.integer(sub(\"^# ALTERNATIVE NAME ([0-9]+):.*\", \"\\\\1\", name_lines))\nfood_names <- sub(\"^# ALTERNATIVE NAME [0-9]+: \", \"\", name_lines)\nnames(food_names) <- food_ids\n\nballot_lines <- grep(\"^[0-9]+:\", lines, value = TRUE)\ncounts <- as.integer(sub(\":.*\", \"\", ballot_lines))\norders <- lapply(sub(\"^[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*\", \"\", ballot_lines),\n                 function(x) as.integer(strsplit(x, \",\")[[1]]))\nstopifnot(length(food_ids) == 15L, identical(sort(food_ids), seq_len(15L)),\n          !anyDuplicated(food_ids), length(orders) == length(counts),\n          all(!is.na(counts)), all(counts > 0L), sum(counts) == 42L)\nstopifnot(all(vapply(orders, function(x) identical(sort(x), seq_len(15L)), logical(1))))\nexpanded <- vector(\"list\", sum(counts)); k <- 1L\nfor (i in seq_along(orders)) for (j in seq_len(counts[i])) {\n  expanded[[k]] <- orders[[i]]; k <- k + 1L\n}\nballots <- do.call(rbind, expanded)\ncolnames(ballots) <- paste0(\"rank_\", seq_len(ncol(ballots)))\n\nwrite.csv(as.data.frame(ballots), \"/shared/breakfast_ballots.csv\", row.names = FALSE)\nwrite.csv(data.frame(id = food_ids, name = unname(food_names)),\n          \"/shared/breakfast_foods.csv\", row.names = FALSE)\n\nfirst <- sort(table(ballots[, 1]), decreasing = TRUE)\ntop <- head(first, 7)\nshort_names <- c(\"Toast pop-up\", \"Buttered toast\", \"English muffin\", \"Jelly donut\",\n                 \"Cinnamon toast\", \"Blueberry muffin\", \"Hard rolls\", \"Toast + marmalade\",\n                 \"Toast + jelly\", \"Toast + margarine\", \"Cinnamon bun\", \"Danish pastry\",\n                 \"Glazed donut\", \"Coffee cake\", \"Corn muffin\")\nnames(short_names) <- food_ids\nlabels <- short_names[names(top)]\npar(mar = c(4, 10, 3, 1))\nbarplot(rev(top), names.arg = rev(labels), horiz = TRUE, las = 1, col = \"#e8872d\",\n        border = NA, xlab = \"First-choice votes\",\n        main = \"What should accompany cold cereal?\")\ncat(sprintf(\"Validated %d complete rankings of %d foods.\\n\", nrow(ballots), ncol(ballots)))",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "fetch_breakfast_votes"
      },
      {
        "code": "## Python: learn taste profiles and count head-to-head wins\n\nPython reads the files created by R. A tiny deterministic **k-means** model groups similar rankings, while pairwise comparisons create facts in the named `pairwise_model` cell for Prolog to reason over.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "profiles_description"
      },
      {
        "code": ":- dynamic model/1.\nmodel([]).",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "pairwise_model"
      },
      {
        "code": "import csv, hashlib, numpy as np\n\n# The R cell stores canonical LF source bytes; verify SHA-256 before use.\nwith open(\"/shared/breakfast_source.soc\", \"rb\") as f:\n    source_bytes = f.read()\nif hashlib.sha256(source_bytes).hexdigest() != \"7013ed36716e5a2b0c2d7813b0da9cd272cf1bfe90eb66d7d8311485a6a32db0\":\n    raise ValueError(\"Pinned dataset SHA-256 mismatch; run fetch_breakfast_votes and inspect the source.\")\n\nwith open(\"/shared/breakfast_foods.csv\", newline=\"\") as f:\n    foods = {int(r[\"id\"]): r[\"name\"] for r in csv.DictReader(f)}\nwith open(\"/shared/breakfast_ballots.csv\", newline=\"\") as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nm = len(foods)\nif sorted(foods) != list(range(1, 16)) or len(rows) != 42:\n    raise ValueError(\"Expected the pinned 42-voter, 15-food dataset\")\nrankings = np.array([[int(r[f\"rank_{j}\"]) for j in range(1, m + 1)] for r in rows])\nif rankings.shape != (42, m) or not np.all(np.sort(rankings, axis=1) == np.arange(1, m + 1)):\n    raise ValueError(\"Every ballot must be a complete permutation of the food IDs\")\npositions = np.empty_like(rankings)\nfor voter, order in enumerate(rankings):\n    positions[voter, order - 1] = np.arange(m)\n\nwins = (positions[:, :, None] < positions[:, None, :]).sum(axis=0)\nmargin = wins - wins.T\nplurality = np.bincount(rankings[:, 0], minlength=m + 1)[1:]\nborda = (m - 1 - positions).sum(axis=0)\ncondorcet = [i for i in range(m) if np.all(np.delete(wins[i], i) > len(rows) / 2)]\n\n# Three taste profiles, learned locally with deterministic k-means.\nX = (m - 1 - positions) / (m - 1)\nseeds = [0]\nwhile len(seeds) < 3:\n    nearest = np.min(((X[:, None, :] - X[seeds][None, :, :]) ** 2).sum(axis=2), axis=1)\n    seeds.append(int(np.argmax(nearest)))\ncentroids = X[seeds].copy()\nfor _ in range(30):\n    labels = ((X[:, None, :] - centroids[None, :, :]) ** 2).sum(axis=2).argmin(axis=1)\n    updated = centroids.copy()\n    for k in range(3):\n        if np.any(labels == k):\n            updated[k] = X[labels == k].mean(axis=0)  # retain an empty cluster's center\n    if np.allclose(updated, centroids): break\n    centroids = updated\n\ndef prolog_atom(text):\n    return \"'\" + text.replace(\"\\\\\", \"\\\\\\\\\").replace(\"'\", \"''\") + \"'\"\n\nfacts = [f\"food({i},{prolog_atom(foods[i])})\" for i in range(1, m + 1)]\nfor a in range(m):\n    for b in range(m):\n        if margin[a, b] > 0:\n            facts.append(f\"beats({a+1},{b+1},{int(margin[a,b])})\")\nfor k in range(3):\n    favourite = int(np.argmax(centroids[k])) + 1\n    facts.append(f\"taste_bloc({k+1},{int((labels == k).sum())},{favourite})\")\n# Keep all tied plurality/Borda winners instead of choosing the lowest ID.\nfacts += [\n    *[f\"rule_winner(plurality,{i+1})\" for i in np.flatnonzero(plurality == plurality.max())],\n    *[f\"rule_winner(borda,{i+1})\" for i in np.flatnonzero(borda == borda.max())],\n    *[f\"rule_winner(condorcet,{i+1})\" for i in condorcet]\n]\nnb_write(\"pairwise_model\", \".code\", \":- dynamic model/1.\\nmodel([\" + \",\".join(facts) + \"]).\")\n\nprint(\"Plurality winner(s):\", \", \".join(foods[int(i)+1] for i in np.flatnonzero(plurality == plurality.max())))\nprint(\"Borda winner(s)    :\", \", \".join(foods[int(i)+1] for i in np.flatnonzero(borda == borda.max())))\nprint(\"Condorcet winner :\", foods[condorcet[0] + 1] if condorcet else \"none\")\nfor k in range(3):\n    top = np.argsort(centroids[k])[::-1][:3] + 1\n    print(f\"Taste bloc {k+1} ({(labels == k).sum():2d} voters):\", \", \".join(foods[int(i)] for i in top))\nprint(\"Named pairwise model written for Prolog.\")",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "learn_preferences"
      },
      {
        "code": "## Prolog: find a majority cycle\n\nProlog reads Python's named `pairwise_model` source cell. Each `beats(A, B, Margin)` edge records a strict majority preference and the vote-count difference, not the number of people voting for A. Prolog searches for three edges that close into a directed cycle, then reports a cycle with the largest minimum edge margin.\n\nA cycle among some foods can coexist with an overall Condorcet winner. The k-means taste profiles are a separate exploratory summary and play no role in constructing majority edges or choosing voting-rule winners.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "prolog_description"
      },
      {
        "code": ":- dynamic food/2, beats/3, taste_bloc/3, rule_winner/2.\n\nmajority_cycle(A, B, C, AB, BC, CA) :-\n    beats(A, B, AB), beats(B, C, BC), beats(C, A, CA),\n    A < B, A < C.\n\n\\+ \\+ (\n    user:nb_read('pairwise_model', '.code', _ModelSource),\n    split_string(_ModelSource, \"\\n\", \"\\r\", [\":- dynamic model/1.\", _ModelText]),\n    term_string(model(_Facts), _ModelText),\n    ( _Facts == []\n    -> writeln(\"Run fetch_breakfast_votes and learn_preferences before this cell.\"), fail\n    ;  true ),\n    retractall(food(_,_)), retractall(beats(_,_,_)),\n    retractall(taste_bloc(_,_,_)), retractall(rule_winner(_,_)),\n    maplist(assertz, _Facts),\n    findall(_Key-cycle(_A,_B,_C,_AB,_BC,_CA),\n            (majority_cycle(_A,_B,_C,_AB,_BC,_CA), _Min is min(_AB,min(_BC,_CA)), _Key is -_Min),\n            _Cycles),\n    ( _Cycles == []\n    -> writeln(\"No strict three-food majority cycle was found.\"), fail\n    ;  keysort(_Cycles, [_-cycle(_A,_B,_C,_AB,_BC,_CA)|_]) ),\n    food(_A, _NA), food(_B, _NB), food(_C, _NC),\n    format(\"ROCK-PAPER-SCISSORS, SERVED FOR BREAKFAST~n~n\", []),\n    format(\"~w beats ~w by ~d votes~n\", [_NA, _NB, _AB]),\n    format(\"~w beats ~w by ~d votes~n\", [_NB, _NC, _BC]),\n    format(\"~w beats ~w by ~d votes~n~n\", [_NC, _NA, _CA]),\n    forall(rule_winner(_Rule, _Winner),\n           (food(_Winner, _Name), format(\"~w winner: ~w~n\", [_Rule, _Name])))\n).",
        "type": "code",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "find_majority_cycle"
      },
      {
        "code": "## What this shows—and what it does not\n\nEach respondent supplied a complete, internally transitive ranking, but majority comparisons among **some** foods form a cycle. The original pinned data also have an overall Condorcet winner: that food beats every other food head-to-head even though cycles exist among the remaining foods. The plurality winner differs from the Borda and Condorcet winners, showing that the aggregation rule matters.\n\nThis is a concrete illustration of the **Condorcet paradox**—majority preferences need not be transitive. It is not by itself a proof of **Arrow's impossibility theorem**. Arrow's theorem concerns a complete, transitive social ranking with at least three alternatives and unrestricted individual rankings; no such aggregation rule simultaneously satisfies Pareto unanimity, independence of irrelevant alternatives, and non-dictatorship.\n\nThe k-means groups are deterministic descriptions of these ranking vectors, not discovered biological categories, a predictive model, or evidence of stable preferences in a wider population. They depend on the three-cluster choice, numeric rank encoding, Euclidean distance, and initialization.\n\nThe useful conclusion is not that voting is futile. It is that aggregation rules and modeling choices make trade-offs which should be explicit and inspectable.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "interpretation"
      },
      {
        "code": "### Data, provenance, and licensing\n\n**Dataset:** [PrefLib Breakfast Items, series 00035](https://preflib.github.io/PrefLib-Jekyll/dataset/00035), file `00035-00000004.soc` (juice, cold cereal, beverage). The source contains rankings from 42 respondents over 15 foods.\n\n**Immutable source commit:** `6e2e9dcef5ee624383e3e2031f741728dd64d032` in [PrefLib/PrefLib-Data](https://github.com/PrefLib/PrefLib-Data). The R cell downloads only this pinned file, validates its canonical-LF MD5 using base R, and caches it locally. Python validates the canonical source's **SHA-256** before computing results: `7013ed36716e5a2b0c2d7813b0da9cd272cf1bfe90eb66d7d8311485a6a32db0`. These integrity checks prevent silently analyzing changed or malformed input; they are not a signature or independent endorsement of the data.\n\n**Required citation:** Paul E. Green and Vithala R. Rao, *Applied Multidimensional Scaling: A Comparison of Approaches and Algorithms*. Holt, Rinehart and Winston, 1972. Digitization credit: Dominik Peters, as recorded by PrefLib.\n\n**Upstream licence:** the [PrefLib-Data repository licence](https://github.com/PrefLib/PrefLib-Data/blob/6e2e9dcef5ee624383e3e2031f741728dd64d032/LICENSE) is GPL-3.0. This workbook does not bundle or relicense that dataset. Retain its source, licence, and required citation if you redistribute the downloaded file separately.\n\nThe first data fetch sends a normal HTTPS request to GitHub's public raw-file host, which can receive ordinary request/network metadata. After that download, the analyses run locally in SciREPL. No user ballots or workbook content are uploaded.",
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "data_citation"
      }
    ]
  }
}
